{"schema":"cr/v1","tip":"79579688921bd89106753cb758289740278e663e","base":"0970a1051da044cb2d31bc0571aaf446ebd6201f","branch":"wu/E-RGUARD/w3-inbox-channel","committer":"vuluu <quangvu.luu92@gmail.com>","timestamp":1794709396,"verdict":"FAIL","blockers":5,"importants":5,"code_diff_hash":"95f985a4d80da292cd6da94ea53368e24427d41c","round":2,"scope":"full","findings":[{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:77-79","severity":"blocker","claim":"An inbox file whose id is all digits with a leading zero and an 8 or 9 (e.g. 0000000009-x.msg with a matching id: line and a valid kind:) passes message_ok, then show's $(( $(date +%s) - epoch )) aborts with 'value too great for base' under set -eu, so every watch exits 1 with no MSG and no TIMEOUT and every real message behind it starves.","evidence":"Reproduced on a $TMPDIR copy: 0000000009-x.msg plus a real post; watch 3 exits 1 at once with 'line 79: 0000000009: value too great for base', no MSG/TIMEOUT; after consume 0000000009-x the real MSG arrives. id_ok :52 and message_ok :61-69 accept it, glob order :137-143 puts it first, :78 lets a leading-zero all-digit epoch through, /bin/sh is bash (octal). Breaks :17-19 and DEC-57 (router.html:183) 'a hostile name can neither be printed nor starve the queue', and worker-ops.md:206-210's exit 1 = TIMEOUT; tests/router_inbox.rs:309-327 use only octal-valid stems. Not a repeat of b006031's :107-121 finding (different defect: age arithmetic)."},{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:84","severity":"blocker","claim":"The 'more body lines' marker only tests whether file line 86 (body line 81) is non-empty, so a body of 80 lines, a blank line, then more text prints exactly 80 lines with no marker and the router consumes a message whose tail it never saw.","evidence":"Probe: seq 1 80, a blank line, 'TAIL-UNSEEN' posted; watch printed lines 1-80 and no 'more body'/'truncated' marker; the cut test :82 cannot catch it (231 bytes). Present since b006031 and not judged by any earlier entry; tests/router_inbox.rs:346-357 test only 90 lines with no blank line."},{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:82","severity":"blocker","claim":"post accepts a body of exactly maxbytes bytes and stores it with a trailing newline (:116-117, :124), so show's cut test counts maxbytes+1 and prints '… truncated at 4000 bytes' for a message printed in full.","evidence":"Probe: 4000-byte post; watch printed all 4000 bytes (bytes=4000) followed by the truncation marker; 3999 bytes prints none. New off-by-one in 503a41f's cut test; the 5 MiB cap itself holds (tests/router_inbox.rs:431-447)."},{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:158-161","severity":"blocker","claim":"When read/<id>.msg exists while inbox/<id>.msg is also pending (e.g. a consumed message copied back), consume prints CONSUMED-ALREADY and exits 0 without moving the inbox file, so watch re-delivers that message every arm and every message behind it starves.","evidence":"Probe: post two, consume the first, copy read/<id>.msg back; consume <id> printed CONSUMED-ALREADY rc=0, the file stayed, every watch 1 printed the same MSG, status pending=2, the second message never came out. :158-161 returns before :162-166; watch :137-145 never checks read/; no delete verb (:22-23, guard admits only consume). Breaks :8 and :17-19 and DEC-57 junk handling (router.html:183); unsure important/blocker resolved upward."},{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:182","severity":"blocker","claim":"status prints oldest=<id>.msg (the file name) rather than the id, so passing it to consume looks up <id>.msg.msg and fails MISSING, and the status tests at tests/router_inbox.rs:196, 426, 475 pass only because they use a substring contains(\"oldest={id}\").","evidence":"Probe: status printed oldest=1794709278-G1HNkQ.msg; consume of that gave 'MISSING id=1794709278-G1HNkQ.msg' rc=2 (id_ok :52 accepts the dot, :163 looks for .msg.msg). The WU plan's contract is oldest=<id|none> (erguard-w3-inbox-plan.html:117) and MSG id= (:87) prints the bare id; unsure important/blocker resolved upward."},{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:67-68","severity":"important","claim":"The byte-cap fix bounds only what is printed, not what is read: message_ok's sed -n '1s/^id: //p' and '3s/^kind: //p' (and show's :75/:76/:84 seds) have no q, so every poll (every 2 s for up to 6600 s) and every status call reads each candidate *.msg to end of file, a cost that grows with file size.","evidence":"Measured on a 45 MB junk file with a passing id and bad kind: per-sed cost grows with size versus 0.00 s with q; status over two such files took 0.60 s; efficiency finder measured 100 MB 0.04 s, 300 MB 0.09 s per call. Any local process may write the inbox (DEC-57). tests/router_inbox.rs:431-447 asserts output size only. Raised by correctness and efficiency finders; not a repeat of b006031's printed-bytes finding."},{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:137","severity":"important","claim":"Each idle poll re-runs message_ok (expr plus one or two seds in subshells) on every leftover non-message *.msg, with no memo between polls and no verb the router can use to clear such files, so an idle watch's CPU cost grows linearly with the leftover count for the whole 6600 s arm.","evidence":"Measured on a $TMPDIR copy: watch 0 on an empty inbox 0.017 s; with 200 well-named junk files (kind BAD) 0.98 s wall / 0.8 s CPU per scan, about 40% CPU against the 2 s poll. Guard router-guard.jq:35 has no delete verb; status :179-192 does not count junk so the router cannot see it; DEC-57 (router.html:185) leaves the count unbounded. Raised from nit."},{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:82","severity":"important","claim":"show runs three separate tail -n +6 | head -c pipelines over the same body (:82, :86, :88), with :86 and :88 duplicating the head|sed 1,80p|clean chain, so the cap rule lives in three copies that must agree and already disagree (the :82 copy counts the stored trailing newline the printed copy drops).","evidence":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:82,:86,:88; :85 and DEC-57 promise bytes= is what was printed; the :82/:88 disagreement is the false-truncation blocker above. A duplicated rule that drifts is the rubric's important row; raised from nit."},{"location":"scripts/router-inbox.sh:114","severity":"important","claim":"post's body=$(cat) buffers all of stdin before the byte-limit check at :116-117 refuses it, so an oversized stdin post costs time and memory proportional to its size, and the refusal test at tests/router_inbox.rs:238-248 only exercises an argument body.","evidence":"Probe: 40,000,000 bytes piped to post were read and counted (1.28 s) before 'body is 40000000 bytes; limit is 4000'; the first 4,001 bytes decide it. Rubric P1 (cost grows with the data); the argument path is bounded by ARG_MAX."},{"location":"scripts/router-guard.jq:35","severity":"important","claim":"The consume-id grammar [A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]* and the watch-seconds grammar [0-9]{1,7} are written twice, in the guard and in the script's id_ok (:52) and max check (:132), and no in-range test ties the copies, so tightening either side leaves every test green while the router is denied a real consume <id>/watch <n> or admitted to one the script refuses.","evidence":"Copies are word for word at router-guard.jq:35 and router-inbox.sh:52,:132; tests/router.rs:303-339 check only two hard-coded ids; tests/router_inbox.rs never calls the guard. Current behaviour is consistent (probed a minted id, consume _x, watch 12345678). Rubric important row 'a duplicated rule that will drift'; not a repeat of b006031's settings-vs-guard nit."}],"refuted":[{"location":"tests/init.rs:518-549","claim":"The W3 contract is checked only by exact-phrase contains/!contains on the shipped rule text, so a rewording reds and a dropped rule with a kept phrase stays green, yet the test note counts it as W3 coverage.","why":"Exact-phrase checks of shipped rule text are this test's deliberate convention from W1's rule-text rounds (tests/init.rs:518 comment; commits cf805